description:
FILED s537a (Opus 5.5, 2026-11-10) as #4281's residue.  PRE-EXISTING (main 4a39a1c7 the same).  LOW.

    sub w4 { my $i = 0; while ($i < 5) { $i++; last if $i == 2 } }
    sub c4 { while () { last } }
    sub f3 { my $i = 0; for (;;) { last if ++$i > 2 } }
    sub c13 { my $i = 0; OUT: while (1) { while (1) { last OUT if ++$i > 2 } } }
    my @r = w4(); print scalar(@r), "\n";     # perl 0   PCL 1 (one undef)
    my $s = w4(); print defined $s ? 1 : 0;   # perl 0   PCL 0 (undef: right)

#4281 made a loop that ends by its TEST answer the test's last value (ir-spec §6.2 "THE VALUE OF A LOOP").  A loop ended by `last` (lexical `(return nil)`, labelled `(throw 'LAST-x nil)`, dynamic `(return-from out nil)` in %p-loop-driver) still answers raw NIL, which a list-context sub return reads as ONE undef.  perl's `last` leaves nothing on the stack: the empty list in list context, undef in scalar.

WHY NOT FIXED IN s537a: the loop does not know its own context.  The obvious `(p-return-empty *pcl-caller-wantarray*)` at the exit is WRONG off the sub tail: a loop that is the tail of a `do {}` in an assignment (`my $x = do { while (1) { last } }`) inside a sub called in LIST context would hand `my $x =` an empty adjustable vector, which box-set stores as its COUNT 0 where perl stores undef -- a regression from today's undef.  The fix needs the static context: the compiler knows a tail loop's $tail_ctx (`_lower_while_stmt` / `_lower_cfor_stmt` receive it), so the tail lowering could emit the loop under a marker that turns the `last` exit into `(p-return-empty)` read in the CALLER's context (the `_restore_caller_wa` shape), leaving every non-tail loop byte-identical.  Probe: scratch/s537a/p/m2a.pl, m2c.pl (w4 f3 c4 c13) in the s537a worktree.
